/-!
# A fibrewise dense affine open over a Dedekind domain

Source: Bosch--Lütkebohmert--Raynaud, Néron Models, 6.4 Lemma 4 and the construction
in the proof of Theorem 1, printed pp.155–156
(blueprint: lem-sfb-dedekind-smooth-fibre-dense-affine).

The boundary is represented by its quasi-coherent ideal `E`; its closed subscheme is
`E.subscheme`, and `E.IsInvertible` says locally generated by a non-zero-divisor.
The inverse image under the first fibre-product projection is the base-changed open.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A smooth separated finite type scheme over a Noetherian normal domain of dimension one
has an affine open with effective Cartier boundary, dense in every geometric fibre.

Source: BLR, Néron Models, 6.4 Lemma 4 and proof of Theorem 1, printed pp.155–156
(blueprint: lem-sfb-dedekind-smooth-fibre-dense-affine).

Proof plan (the numbered steps are those of the DAG node):
1. Localize the base at its primes. Noetherian normal local domains of dimension one are
regular; the localization at zero is a field (lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian,
lem-weil-pbc-localization-normal-domain, lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-dim-one-iff-normal).
2. Finite type over the Noetherian base makes X Noetherian. Smooth ascent of regularity
and regular implies normal split X into finitely many integral clopen regular components
(lem-weil-pbc-regular-ring-smooth-ascent, lem-weil-pbc-regular-ring-normal,
lem-trf-normal-topologically-noetherian-components). If X is empty use the empty open
and the unit ideal.
3. Smoothness gives flatness. The flat local dimension formula, with the fibre stalk
quotient, shows that generic points of closed fibres have local dimension one in X,
and generic-fibre generic points have dimension zero (lem-pc-smooth-flat,
lem-weil-pbc-flat-height-formula, lem-sbc-scheme-fibre-stalk-quotient,
lem-sc-scheme-stalk-dimension-generalization-chains).
4. For each closed base point, take an affine open containing all generic points of X
and of its fibre (lem-trf-codimension-one-points-affine-neighbourhood).
5. The finitely many boundary components have codimension one
(lem-sfb-affine-open-boundary-codimension-one). None of the vertical ones lies over the
chosen point, by step 3 and the choice of the affine open.
6. Remove their images and shrink to a basic base neighbourhood of the chosen point.
The corresponding restricted open stays affine, being principal. Quasi-compactness
of the base gives finitely many such neighbourhoods covering it
(lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open, lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs).
7. Take the finite union H of the closures of all horizontal boundary components.
Each retains its generic point and codimension one, and H contains each local boundary.
8. H contains no fibre component: in a closed fibre this would yield a strict chain of
length two below a point of local dimension one; in the generic fibre it contradicts
local dimension zero (lem-sc-scheme-stalk-dimension-generalization-chains).
Thus X minus H is dense in every residue-field fibre.
9. Give each component of H its reduced induced closed structure. On its regular integral
clopen component it is effective Cartier, and extend by the unit ideal on other components
(lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme,
lem-per-regular-prime-divisor-local-equations).
10. Multiply the resulting ideals. On a common affine neighbourhood the product of their
regular generators is regular. Its support is H, including the empty product case
(lem-weil-pbc-product-ideal-closed-subscheme-union, lem-sc-affine-inter-principal-both,
def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal).
11. On each chosen base neighbourhood the complement lies in the chosen affine open and
is the complement of an effective Cartier divisor there, hence is affine. The target-local
affine criterion shows that the whole complement is affine over the affine base and thus
affine (pqf-lem-affine-local-target, lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence,
lem-weil-pbc-open-preimage-fibre-product).
12. Density in residue-field fibres passes to the geometric fibres, including empty ones
(lem-sfb-smooth-fibrewise-dense-open-base-change). No group structure or section is used. -/
theorem exists_isAffineOpen_geometric_fibre_dense_cartier_compl
    (D : Type u) [CommRing D] [IsDomain D] [IsNoetherianRing D] [IsIntegrallyClosed D]
    (hdim : ringKrullDim D = 1) {X : Scheme.{u}} (p : X ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of D))
    [Smooth p] [IsSeparated p] [LocallyOfFiniteType p] [QuasiCompact p] :
    ∃ (A : X.Opens) (E : X.IdealSheafData),
      IsAffineOpen A ∧ E.IsInvertible ∧ (E.support : Set X) = (A : Set X)ᶜ ∧
      ∀ (Ω : Type u) [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
        (s : Spec (CommRingCat.of Ω) ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of D)),
        closure ((pullback.fst p s) ⁻¹' (A : Set X)) = Set.univ := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
